is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_dist_2_1
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in the OSPF control plane due to an area mismatch on the uplink of switch_dist_2_1. After the change window, the OSPF process on switch_dist_2_1 was configured to advertise its core-facing link (172.16.0.8/31) in area 0, while router_core_2 still runs that same link in area 1. Evidence: on switch_dist_2_1, “router ospf … network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0” is present (frr running-config), and “show ip ospf interface” shows eth0 in Area 0.0.0.0 with Neighbor Count 0 and State Waiting. In contrast, router_core_2’s OSPF config retains “network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1,” and the physical link on both sides is up (switch_dist_2_1 eth0 172.16.0.9/31; router_core_2 eth2 172.16.0.8/31).

Mechanism: OSPF refuses Hellos that don’t match key parameters (area ID among them), so neighbors never progress beyond Down, leaving switch_dist_2_1 with no adjacency to the core. With no OSPF peering, routes to the rest of the network (pod 1 and server farm) aren’t exchanged, so host_2_1_1_1 can reach its default gateway (10.2.1.1 on br0) but times out beyond it.

Localization: switch_dist_2_1, OSPF on interface eth0 (172.16.0.9/31) toward router_core_2:eth2 (172.16.0.8/31).

Root cause: OSPF area on switch_dist_2_1’s uplink was changed to area 0, creating an area mismatch with router_core_2 (area 1).

Corrective fix: On switch_dist_2_1, change eth0’s network statement to area 1 (no network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0; network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1), then verify “show ip ospf neighbor” forms FULL with router_core_2 and confirm reachability from host_2_1_1_1.=== END ===